# Sidecar image built on REAL Chrome instead of Alpine's chromium.
#
# ⛔⛔ CORRECTION 2026-10-15, AND IT INVALIDATES THIS FILE'S ORIGINAL CHOICE:
# BRANDED GOOGLE CHROME CANNOT LOAD OUR EXTENSION AT ALL. Measured in this very
# image (Chrome 155.0.8059.39), from Chrome's own stderr:
#     WARNING:chrome/browser/extensions/extension_service.cc:423]
#     --load-extension is not allowed in Google Chrome, ignoring.
# It is a branded-build refusal, not a flag we got wrong: the browser starts, X
# starts, the profile is written, and the extension is simply never loaded — so
# nothing ever registers on :9010 and the sidecar is undriveable. The extension
# id appears in the profile's Preferences (server.js seeds it for the incognito
# grant), which makes the failure look like a load that went wrong rather than
# one that never happened.
# ⚠️ HOW THIS GOT PAST THE FIRST BUILD: the -13b container was never given
# UNIBROWSE_AUTOLAUNCH_PROFILE, so it launched no browser and the absence of a
# Chrome process was read as "the image is broken". That absence was CORRECT
# BEHAVIOUR for the env it was given, and it hid the real fault for two days.
# ⇒ A container that was never asked to do the thing is not evidence that it
#   cannot.
#
# ⭐ SO THE BINARY IS CHROME FOR TESTING, not google-chrome-stable. Measured
# 2026-10-15 through the full sidecar path (autolaunch → Xvfb → extension →
# registration on :9010 → browser_evaluate):
#   · --load-extension is HONOURED (no refusal line) and the extension REGISTERS
#   · webgl2=YES, RENDERER=ANGLE (Google, Vulkan 1.3.0 (SwiftShader Device
#     (Subzero)), SwiftShader driver) — but ONLY with the two flags below
#   · TZ=America/New_York resolves; Intl reports it and the offset is 240
#   · ⚠️ navigator.userAgentData.brands = [Chromium/155, Not(A:Brand/24].
#     CfT carries NO "Google Chrome" brand entry, so fault (2) below is NOT
#     fixed by this image — it is narrowed to one field. Closing it needs the
#     extension force-installed from a signed CRX under a Chrome policy, which
#     in turn needs a `key` in extension/manifest.json — and that CHANGES THE
#     UNPACKED EXTENSION ID (today it is derived from the directory PATH,
#     server.js:327), which every existing profile has stored. Not a rush job.
#
# ⛔ WEBGL UNDER Xvfb NEEDS TWO FLAGS AND IS SILENT WITHOUT THEM. Measured
# 2026-10-15: the same CfT binary gives webgl2=YES under `--headless=new` and
# webgl2=NO in a real Xvfb window, because ANGLE wants a Vulkan window surface
# (VK_KHR_xcb_surface) that SwiftShader's ICD does not provide, and modern
# Chrome gates the software fallback. Both are required:
#     --use-angle=swiftshader --enable-unsafe-swiftshader
# They are baked into UNIBROWSE_CHROME_EXTRA_ARGS below so a deployment that
# forgets them cannot quietly lose WebGL. ⚠️ This also means the Alpine-image
# finding "no flag fixes WebGL" does NOT transfer: that test ran on an image
# with no SwiftShader library present at all.
#
# ⭐ WHY THIS EXISTS — three fingerprint faults, one cause, measured 2026-10-13
# on the live Alpine sidecar (`unibrowse-chromium-host`):
#
#   1. NO WebGL AT ALL. `webgl`, `webgl2` and `experimental-webgl` all return
#      null while canvas 2D works. A desktop browser with zero WebGL is far
#      stranger to an anti-bot gate than a driven one, because no real desktop
#      presents that way.
#   2. It identifies as CHROMIUM, not Google Chrome — `navigator.userAgentData
#      .brands` reads ["Not;A=Brand/8", "Chromium/150"]. Real Chrome carries a
#      "Google Chrome" brand entry.
#   3. NO tzdata. `/usr/share/zoneinfo` is absent, so the container reports UTC
#      and setting TZ alone leaves it on UTC while LOOKING fixed.
#
# ⛔ NO FLAG FIXES (1). Measured, not assumed — all three candidate flag sets
# were tested and all three failed:
#     --use-gl=angle --use-angle=gl          -> WEBGL=NO
#     --use-gl=egl                           -> WEBGL=NO
#     --use-gl=angle --use-angle=swiftshader -> WEBGL=NO
# The non-headless run named the cause in its own log:
#     Extension not supported: VK_KHR_surface / VK_KHR_xcb_surface
#     eglInitialize SwANGLE failed with EGL_NOT_INITIALIZED
#     Exiting GPU process due to errors during initialization
# `libvulkan.so.1` is present but there is no usable Vulkan ICD and no
# `libvk_swiftshader.so` anywhere in the image. Alpine's chromium package
# simply does not ship SwiftShader. mesa's `swrast_dri.so` IS present and does
# not help — ANGLE wants Vulkan, not DRI.
#
# ⭐ CONTROL that proves it is the browser and not the method: office's host
# `google-chrome`, same headless --dump-dom probe, same machine, no GPU:
#     WEBGL=YES via webgl2
#     RENDERER=ANGLE (Google, Vulkan 1.3.0 (SwiftShader Device (Subzero)),
#                     SwiftShader driver)
# Google Chrome BUNDLES its own SwiftShader Vulkan driver, so WebGL works with
# no flags, no GPU and no configuration. ⇒ Fixing (1) and (2) is one change:
# ship real Chrome. (3) comes free from a Debian base.
#
# ⛔⛔ WHY NOT JUST PATCH THE ALPINE IMAGE: it cannot be rebuilt at all. Its
# Dockerfile pins chromium=150.0.7871.181-r0 and alpine 3.24 no longer offers
# that package, so `compose build chromium-host` FAILS. That failure is
# deliberate (read Dockerfile's header: an unpinned rebuild once moved chromium
# 148 -> 149 silently and cost three weeks of undiagnosable macro failure).
# This file is the deliberate version decision that situation has been owed.
#
# ⚠️ HONEST LIMIT ON THE PIN. Google's apt repo serves ONLY current stable, so
# `CHROME_VERSION` pins what we ASKED for and the build records what it GOT —
# it cannot resurrect an old version once Google rotates it. That is weaker
# than the Alpine pin was, and pretending otherwise would recreate exactly the
# silent-drift failure the other Dockerfile was written to prevent.
# ⇒ For true reproducibility, cache the .deb (see CHROME_DEB_URL below) and
# build from that instead. Do it before relying on this image in production.
#
# ⚠️ BUILD FROM THE REPO ROOT, not from chromium-host/ — this image needs
# extension/ which lives at the root, so the context must contain both:
#   docker build -f chromium-host/Dockerfile.chrome \
#     -t 192.168.1.168:32000/unibrowse/chromium-host-chrome:<tag> .
#   docker push 192.168.1.168:32000/unibrowse/chromium-host-chrome:<tag>
# ⛔ BUT THE MANIFEST NAMES IT .207:5050/unibrowse/chromium-host-chrome, and
# that is deliberate — .207:5050 is the cluster's canonical registry name and
# each node tries server4 (.168:32000) first for it, keeping the NAS as a
# fallback. Pushing to the server4 mirror under the SAME repository path is
# what makes a canonical-name pull find it. Full mechanism, and the honest
# limit (the NAS has no copy, so the fallback path exists without the fallback
# bytes), in the long note in k8s/chromium-sidecar.yaml.
# ⛔ Push from office; do NOT build on cluster nodes (cluster, 2026-10-13).
#
# ⭐ MEASURED IN THE BUILT IMAGE 2026-10-13, all three faults fixed:
#   WEBGL=YES via webgl2
#   RENDERER=ANGLE (Google, Vulkan 1.3.0 (SwiftShader Device (Subzero)), SwiftShader driver)
#   UA_BRANDS=[{"brand":"Google Chrome","version":"155"}, {"Chromium","155"}, …]
#   TZ=America/New_York, `date` reads EDT, /usr/share/zoneinfo present
#   libvk_swiftshader.so present (3,980,232 bytes)
# ⭐ AND THE VERSION PIN HELD: the build installed
#   google-chrome-stable_155.0.8059.39-1_amd64.deb — the exact version asked
#   for, not the fallback. The weakness documented above is real but did not
#   bite this build.

ARG NODE_BASE=node:22-bookworm-slim
FROM ${NODE_BASE}

# The version we ASK for. The build prints what it actually installed; if they
# differ, Google has rotated stable and that is a fact to record, not to ignore.
# 155.0.8059.39 = what office's own host Chrome runs as of 2026-10-13, chosen so
# the sidecars stop diverging from the host browser (the host was on Chrome 155
# while the sidecars were on Chromium 150 — two different fingerprints).
ARG CHROME_VERSION=155.0.8059.39
# Set this to a cached .deb URL to make the build reproducible. When set it
# takes precedence over the apt path above.
ARG CHROME_DEB_URL=

ENV DEBIAN_FRONTEND=noninteractive

# ⭐ tzdata is listed FIRST because it is fault (3) and it is the one that fails
# silently: without it TZ=America/New_York is accepted and ignored.
RUN apt-get update && apt-get install -y --no-install-recommends \
      tzdata \
      ca-certificates curl gnupg unzip \
      xvfb \
      xdotool imagemagick xclip x11-utils python3 procps \
      fonts-liberation fonts-noto-color-emoji \
      libnss3 libfreetype6 libharfbuzz0b \
      tini \
    && rm -rf /var/lib/apt/lists/*

RUN set -eux; \
    if [ -n "${CHROME_DEB_URL}" ]; then \
      curl -fsSL -o /tmp/chrome.deb "${CHROME_DEB_URL}"; \
    else \
      curl -fsSL -o /usr/share/keyrings/google-chrome.asc \
        https://dl.google.com/linux/linux_signing_key.pub; \
      echo "deb [arch=amd64 signed-by=/usr/share/keyrings/google-chrome.asc] https://dl.google.com/linux/chrome/deb/ stable main" \
        > /etc/apt/sources.list.d/google-chrome.list; \
      apt-get update; \
      apt-get download "google-chrome-stable=${CHROME_VERSION}-1" \
        || { echo "⚠️ ${CHROME_VERSION} not in Google's repo (they serve only current stable) — taking current and RECORDING it"; \
             apt-get download google-chrome-stable; }; \
      mv google-chrome-stable_*.deb /tmp/chrome.deb; \
    fi; \
    apt-get install -y --no-install-recommends /tmp/chrome.deb; \
    rm -f /tmp/chrome.deb; \
    rm -rf /var/lib/apt/lists/*; \
    # ⭐ Record what we ACTUALLY got, in the image, so a future bisect has a
    # reference point that does not depend on anyone's memory.
    google-chrome --version | tee /etc/unibrowse-chrome-version; \
    test -f /opt/google/chrome/libvk_swiftshader.so \
      || echo "⚠️ libvk_swiftshader.so not where expected — verify WebGL before trusting this image"

# ⭐ CHROME FOR TESTING — the binary we actually drive. Branded Chrome above
# stays installed because its .deb is what pulls in the shared-library set CfT
# also needs, and because it remains the control for "is this Chrome or is this
# us" questions. It is not the browser the sidecar launches.
#
# ⭐ THIS PIN IS REAL, UNLIKE THE APT ONE. The CfT bucket keeps every version
# forever at an immutable URL, so this build is reproducible in a way the
# google-chrome-stable apt path can never be (Google serves only current
# stable). That closes the "HONEST LIMIT ON THE PIN" admission above for the
# binary we actually use.
ARG CFT_VERSION=155.0.8059.79
RUN set -eux; \
    curl -fsSL -o /tmp/cft.zip \
      "https://storage.googleapis.com/chrome-for-testing-public/${CFT_VERSION}/linux64/chrome-linux64.zip"; \
    unzip -q /tmp/cft.zip -d /tmp/cft; \
    mv /tmp/cft/chrome-linux64 /opt/cft; \
    rm -rf /tmp/cft.zip /tmp/cft; \
    /opt/cft/chrome --version | tee /etc/unibrowse-cft-version; \
    test -f /opt/cft/libvk_swiftshader.so \
      || { echo "⛔ libvk_swiftshader.so missing from CfT — WebGL would be off"; exit 1; }

# ⚠️ CHROME_PATH is read by server.js. Points at Chrome for Testing: branded
# Chrome refuses --load-extension (see the correction at the top of this file).
ENV CHROME_PATH=/opt/cft/chrome
# ⛔ REQUIRED FOR WEBGL UNDER Xvfb, and silent if removed. See the header.
# A deployment may override this, and overriding it without these two flags
# turns WebGL off with no error anywhere.
ENV UNIBROWSE_CHROME_EXTRA_ARGS="--use-angle=swiftshader --enable-unsafe-swiftshader"
# Fault (3). Overridable per-deployment; the point is that a named zone now
# RESOLVES, because tzdata is installed above.
ENV TZ=America/New_York

WORKDIR /app

# ⚠️ In compose, server.js and extension/ are BIND-MOUNTED from the repo, so a
# code change needs only a container recreate. A K8s pod has no such mount, so
# both are baked in here and an extension change becomes an image rebuild.
# That is a real tradeoff, accepted deliberately for the cluster deployment.
COPY chromium-host/server.js ./server.js
COPY extension ./extension
# ⭐ THE OS-INPUT LANE SHIPS IN THE SAME IMAGE, DELIBERATELY. `freelance` drives
# Fiverr, Upwork and Freelancer with NO extension and NO debugger — XTEST input
# and X screenshots only — because Fiverr's PerimeterX tripped on extension
# navigation and Upwork blocked extension browsers at Cloudflare (Ben,
# #freelance 2026-10-12 18:26). That lane needs BRANDED google-chrome, which is
# installed above and which is useless for the extension lane for the exact
# reason recorded at the top of this file. One image, two binaries, and which
# one you get is CHROME_PATH (sidecar) or OSDRIVE_CHROME (os-input).
# ⛔ Set OSDRIVE_PROFILE_ROOT to a LOCAL volume. A Chrome profile on NFS
# corrupts — LevelDB and SQLite both.
COPY scripts/osdrive.sh /usr/local/bin/osdrive.sh
RUN chmod +x /usr/local/bin/osdrive.sh
ENV OSDRIVE_PROFILE_ROOT=/app/.chrome-profiles \
    OSDRIVE_SHOT_DIR=/app/shots \
    OSDRIVE_CHROME=/usr/bin/google-chrome

EXPOSE 9020

# ⛔ /usr/bin/tini, NOT /sbin/tini. MEASURED 2026-10-13: the first build of this
# image shipped the Alpine path, carried over from chromium-host/Dockerfile where
# tini is an apk at /sbin/tini. On Debian it is a dpkg at /usr/bin/tini, so every
# container died instantly with
#   OCI runtime create failed: unable to start container process:
#   exec: "/sbin/tini": stat /sbin/tini: no such file or directory
# and the pod CrashLoopBackOff'd on server3 after a perfectly good 416 MB pull.
# ⚠️ HOW IT GOT PAST ME: I "probed inside the image" and reported WebGL, the UA
# brands, the timezone and the Chrome version as proof it was good — by running
# chrome directly with `docker run --entrypoint`. That never executes ENTRYPOINT,
# so it proved the BROWSER worked and said nothing about whether the CONTAINER
# could start. A control that tests the step before the one in doubt reads as
# confirmation and is worth nothing. ⇒ Verify a container image by RUNNING it,
# not by running something inside it.
ENTRYPOINT ["/usr/bin/tini", "--"]
CMD ["node", "server.js"]
